{
  "skillId": "oop-advanced",
  "title": "Inheritance and polymorphism",
  "minutes": 5,
  "version": 1,
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "hook",
      "type": "hook",
      "title": "One call, many behaviours",
      "body": "A payment app has card, UPI and wallet payments, and they all *pay*, just differently. With **inheritance** they share a parent type, and with **polymorphism** one line of code calls the right version for each. Interviewers ask about this constantly.\n\nIn five minutes you’ll see Java pick the method at run time, and wire up a subclass yourself."
    },
    {
      "id": "p1",
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this program print?",
      "code": "class Animal {\n    String sound() {\n        return \"...\";\n    }\n}\n\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    @Override\n    String sound() {\n        return \"Woof\"; //~ Dog replaces (overrides) Animal's sound().\n    }\n}\n\nclass Cat extends Animal {\n    @Override\n    String sound() {\n        return \"Meow\";\n    }\n}\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Animal[] pets = { new Dog(), new Cat(), new Animal() }; //~ One array of Animals holding a Dog, a Cat and a plain Animal.\n        for (Animal p : pets) {\n            System.out.println(p.sound()); //~ Java calls the sound() of the ACTUAL object. That is polymorphism.\n        }\n    }\n}",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Woof\nMeow\n...",
          "why": "Right. Each object uses its own class’s sound(), even through an Animal variable."
        },
        {
          "text": "It doesn't compile",
          "why": "A Dog IS an Animal, so it fits in an Animal array."
        },
        {
          "text": "...\n...\n...",
          "why": "The variable’s type is Animal, but Java runs the method of the real object inside it."
        },
        {
          "text": "Woof\nMeow",
          "why": "There are three objects. The plain Animal says \"...\"."
        }
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
