    {
      "id": "ideas",
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "Inheritance in four ideas",
      "points": [
        "`class Dog extends Animal`: Dog **inherits** Animal’s fields and methods.",
        "A subclass can **override** a method (`@Override`) to specialise it.",
        "A parent-type variable can hold a child object, and **the object decides** which method runs.",
        "`abstract` classes and `interface`s say *what* must exist and leave *how* to each subclass."
      ],
      "pattern": "abstract class Shape {\n    abstract double area();\n}\nclass Circle extends Shape {\n    double r;\n    double area() { return 3.14 * r * r; }\n}"
    },
    {
      "id": "c1",
      "type": "concept",
      "ref": "OOP027",
      "question": "Methods can be overloaded by changing only the return type.",
      "options": [
        "True",
        "False"
      ],
      "answer": 1,
      "explanation": "Overloads must differ in parameter list (number, type, or order). Return type alone is not enough.",
      "topic": "Polymorphism"
    },
    {
      "id": "p2",
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "A classic trap. What does this print?",
      "code": "class Base {\n    String hello() {\n        return \"Base\";\n    }\n}\n\nclass Child extends Base {\n    @Override\n    String hello() {\n        return \"Child+\" + super.hello();\n    }\n}\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Base x = new Child();\n        System.out.println(x.hello());\n    }\n}",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Child+Base",
          "why": "Yes. Child’s version runs, and super.hello() adds the parent’s text."
        },
        {
          "text": "It doesn't compile",
          "why": "A Base variable may hold a Child; that is exactly how polymorphism works."
        },
